  "text": "Vashti said no to the king and kept her crown out of it. Picture it. Day seven of a royal drinking festival, and the king's heart is merry with wine. He sends seven of his chamberlains to fetch Queen Vashti, so the princes and the people can look at her, because the text says she was fair to look on. And Vashti says no. She refuses to come. Now, Scripture does not tell us why. It records her refusal and not her reasoning, so anyone who tells you exactly what she was thinking is filling in a blank. What we do get is the reaction. The king is furious. His advisers panic, and one of them stands up and says, in effect, if this gets out, every woman in the empire will hear about it and start answering back. So they write a law. They take her royal estate and give it to another. They send letters into every province, in every language, saying that every man should bear rule in his own house. That is how threatened they were. One woman said one word, and an empire wrote legislation about it.",
